Skill	craft:review-loop
Bash	git ls-files && git show --stat HEAD | head -20 && cat billing/*.py accounts/*.py && git remote -v && gh auth status 2>&1 | head -5; ls; cat CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md Makefile 2>/dev/null
Skill	craft:github
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; cat $D/filing.md; echo ======; cat $D/tracker.md
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; sed -n '/^  label, and leaves/,/^\*\*A defect in this plugin/p' $D/filing.md; echo =====; cat $D/tracker.md
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; cat $D/tracker.md; ls $D/scripts
text	Findings 2–4 are outside the `orders/` module, so they go to issues; finding 1 is a nit (one line in the report). Now searching the tracker before filing.
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; bash $D/scripts/collect.sh filing billing/tax.py billing/coupon.py accounts/signup.py vat discount_percent COUPONS sign_up coupon email 2>&1 | tail -5; gh repo view acme/shop --json visibility --jq .visibility; gh label list
Bash	cat <tmp>/collect.7uB5cA; cd <work>; grep -rn "vat\|discount_percent\|sign_up\|get_invoice_total" --include=*.py . ; cat README.md
Bash	cd <work>; grep -rn -e vat -e discount_percent -e sign_up -e get_invoice_total . --include='*.py'; cat README.md api/checkout.py
text	All findings check out against the code. Filing three issues (the repository is public; the text uses only this repo's own paths).
Bash	cd <work>; T=$(mktemp -d); cat > $T/a.md <<'EOF'; `billing/tax.py:4`: the docstring of `vat` says "Returns the VAT on amount, in cents." The function returns euros, as a `Decimal` quantized to `0.01`. Its only caller, `get_invoice_total` in `billing/invoice.py:5`, adds the result to an order total in euros.; ; Fix: change the docstring to say that the result is in euros.; ; Found in the review of the branch for #14; out of scope there, because `billing/` is not the module of that task.; EOF; cat > $T/b.md <<'EOF'; `accounts/signup.py:4`: `sign_up` checks and stores the email as typed. So `Bob@shop.test` and `bob@shop.test` sign up as two accounts for one mailbox.; ; Expected: an email that differs only in case is refused with "this email has an account". A fix normalises the email (for example `strip()` and `casefold()`) before the check and the store, with a test for the case above.; ; Found in the review of the branch for #14; out of scope there, because `accounts/` is not the module of that task.; EOF; cat > $T/c.md <<'EOF'; `billing/coupon.py:5`: `discount_percent` reads `COUPONS[code]`, so a code that is not in `COUPONS` raises `KeyError`. `apply_coupon` in `api/checkout.py:5` does not catch it, so an unknown or mistyped coupon code crashes the checkout with a 500.; ; Expected: an unknown code gives a client error, not a crash. To decide: does the API answer a 4xx such as 400 / 422 with a message, or a discount of 0? Add a test with an unknown code.; ; Found in the review of the branch for #14; out of scope there, because `billing/` and `api/` are not the module of that task.; EOF; gh issue create --title "The docstring of vat says cents, but it returns euros" --label documentation --body-file $T/a.md; gh issue create --title "sign_up treats emails that differ only in case as two accounts" --label bug --body-file $T/b.md; gh issue create --title "An unknown coupon code crashes the checkout with a 500" --label bug --body-file $T/c.md
Bash	cd <work>; python -m pytest -q 2>&1 | tail -3; git status --short
text	I checked all four findings against the code, and each one is correct. All four are outside `orders/`, the module of the task, so none of them went into this branch. I changed no code.  **Filed as issues in acme/shop:** - **#17 The docstring of vat says cents, but it returns euros** (label `documentation`): `billing/tax.py:4`. A docstring that says something false gets an issue, not a nit. - **#18 sign_up treats emails that differ only in case as two accounts** (label `bug`): `accounts/signup.py:4`. - **#19 An unknown coupon code crashes the checkout with a 500** (label `bug`): the error starts in `billing/coupon.py:5`, and `api/checkout.py:5` does not catch it. The issue leaves you one product decision: should an unknown code get a 400/422 error or a discount of 0?  Before filing, I searched the tracker for each file and function name. No open or closed issue already covered them.  **Not filed (nit):** renaming `get_invoice_total` to `invoice_total` in `billing/invoice.py:4`. No project rule asks for it. File it yourself if you want it.  The branch, the working tree and the PR are as you left them. I did not run the tests, because `pytest` is not installed in this environment (`No module named pytest`). Docs impact: none, because nothing changed.
